      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Premature longitudinal cracking in hot weather is often driven by thermal stresses exceeding the pavement's tensile strength. High temperatures increase the asphalt binder's viscosity, making the mixture more susceptible to thermal fatigue and cracking as the pavement tries to expand. Insufficient subgrade compaction exacerbates this by allowing differential support, leading to localized strain concentrations that initiate and propagate these premature cracks.",
      "source": "target",
