    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if the binder is getting too stiff to handle the expansion, what role does the subgrade play in the crack propagation? i'm worried that if the compaction isn't uniform, it's going to make the thermal stress issues even worse. can you explain how a poor subgrade might accelerate the longitudinal cracking in these high-heat conditions?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A poorly compacted subgrade acts as an uneven foundation, forcing the pavement to experience non-uniform support during thermal expansion. When the subgrade settles or deflects unevenly, it creates localized areas of high vertical and horizontal strain within the asphalt layer. This lack of consistent restraint concentrates the stresses from the stiff, heat-affected binder into specific planes, significantly accelerating the initiation and propagation of longitudinal cracks.",
      "source": "target",
